        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Hills, beaches and the Valley: where the glass goes</strong></p><p>In the Hollywood Hills, Laurel Canyon, Silver Lake, Mount Washington and Bel Air, homes climb the slope, and the deck is where you live. Those decks face the canyon or the city lights, and a frameless glass deck railing keeps the view clear. On the Westside and in the South Bay, Venice, Marina del Rey and Manhattan Beach have roof terraces and walk-street balconies. Condos on the marina sit in salt air all year.</p><p>Pacific Palisades and the Santa Monica Mountains sit in very high fire hazard severity zones. So do the San Gabriel foothills above Pasadena and La Cañada Flintridge. Inland, the San Fernando Valley, Pasadena and the San Gabriel Valley are pool-deck country, with hot summers and glass pool fences around backyard pools. Downtown, condo balconies and rooftop amenity decks use the same panels at 42 inches.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>LADBS permits, hillside rules and the 42-inch guard</strong></p><p>Inside the City of Los Angeles, permits go through the Los Angeles Department of Building and Safety (LADBS). Hillside lots fall under the city's hillside rules, which add review for a new deck. Santa Monica, Manhattan Beach, Pasadena, Beverly Hills and Glendale are separate cities with their own building departments. Altadena and Marina del Rey are unincorporated and go through Los Angeles County.</p><p>The California code sets a 42-inch minimum guard height, even on a house (CRC R312.1). The state pool safety act sets a 60-inch enclosure for backyard pools. Gaps must be under 4 inches, and the latch at least 60 inches above the ground. In very high fire hazard severity zones, the code has rules for exterior building elements. Glass and stainless steel do not burn, but check with LADBS and the fire authority about the deck itself. See the <a href=\"/pages/california-building-standards-code-for-glass-railings\">California code page</a> and the <a href=\"/pages/glass-railing-california\">California hub</a>.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Sun, salt and canyon roads: materials and delivery</strong></p><p>Los Angeles has two climates. The Westside and the beach cities get a marine layer and salt air. The Valley, Pasadena and the inland basin get long, dry summers and Santa Ana winds in the fall. Our hardware is 2205 marine-grade stainless steel with a 10-year no-rust promise, which matters in Marina del Rey and Manhattan Beach. The 12 mm tempered glass does not fade in Valley heat. Both finishes, Brushed Stainless and Matte Black, are on the same steel.</p><p>Freight delivery takes 5–10 business days after 2–3 business days of processing, with a liftgate at the curb. Canyon roads in Laurel Canyon, Beachwood Canyon and Topanga are narrow and steep, so tell us where a truck can stop when you order. Inspect the crate and report any damage within 3 days, before installation, for a free replacement or refund. Shipping is free on orders over 3,000 USD.</p>"
          }
        }
